  "updated": "2026-10-27T13:14:40.757102+00:00",
  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nFriday is still looking like a fairly wet day across the bulk of\ncentral Arizona as moisture levels rise to 200-250% of normal and\nthe area falls under strong difluence aloft due to the incoming\nnear record strength (for this time of year) subtropical jet.\nGuidance continues to heavily favor showers and isolated\nthunderstorms developing at some point early Friday morning\ncentered over south-central Arizona and then expanding northward\nand eastward throughout the day on Friday. Rainfall rates are not\nanticipated to be all that heavy with the bulk of the rainfall\nfalling at less than 0.1\" per hour, but with embedded heavier\nshowers or thunderstorms, rates upwards of 0.5-1.0\" per hour\nshould be possible. GEFS and EPS ensemble mean QPF for the Friday\nevent shows an average of around 0.50\"-0.75\" from Phoenix and \ninto the higher terrain north and northeast of Phoenix to \n0.10-0.25\" over much of the rest of southern and central Arizona.\nThe higher end of guidance (NBM 75% percentile) still shows \npotential for heavy rainfall with amounts of up to around 1.5\" \nacross south-central Arizona. As a result, WPC's ERO continues to \ndepict a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall on Friday. Given the \npropensity for the NBM to forecast too much QPF for our area in \nthe mid to long range, we are leaning toward the lower end \namounts. Some higher end amounts of over 1\" are expected to fall,\nbut it is likely to be fairly localized. \n\nAs the subtropical jet lifts northeastward later on Friday, the \nbest ascent will shift out of Arizona leaving only continued \nchances for isolated upslope driven shower activity for Friday \nnight and Saturday. The unsettled weather pattern will persist \nover the weekend with an atmospheric river approaching the region \nfrom the southwest later on Saturday and likely arriving during \nthe day Sunday with the brunt of this expected modest atmospheric \nriver impacting areas across southern Arizona and northern \nSonora. Model uncertainty remains quite high for this second \nweather event with differences in the track and the strength of \nthe shortwave trough and differences in the amount of moisture. \nThe EPS continues to show higher moisture levels and QPF amounts \ncompared than the GEFS, but both have trended lower with QPF over \nthe past 24 hours. The GEFS's additional 0.25-0.50\" for south-\ncentral Arizona looks to be a bit too low considering the amount \nof moisture potential, but the EPS's 0.75-1.50\" may be a bit too \nhigh. The timing with the bulk of the rain likely falling during \nthe first half of Sunday still looks good, but as stated above how\nmuch rain will fall is still quite uncertain. Considering this \nwill be the second rain event within 3 days, any additional \nmoderate to heavy rain could compound any flooding issues that \ncould occur on Friday. Rain chances may linger through Sunday \nnight across eastern Arizona, but by Monday much of the area \nshould be drying out as the system is expected to quickly push \nnortheast of the area.\n\nTemperatures during this unsettled weather pattern are expected to\nfall below normal starting Friday with highs dropping to between\n75-80 degrees before bottoming out closer to 70 degree highs for\nSunday and/or Monday. NBM guidance shows fairly large forecast \ntemperature spread for the first half of next week as the large\nscale troughing may linger over the region for a couple more days.\nIf this happens, highs will very slowly creep upward through the\nlow to mid 70s, whereas if the ridge takes over we could see \nhighs closer to 80 degrees by next Tuesday or Wednesday.",
